        "bodyTr": "The Democratic Party is showing signs of confusion over its real estate tax reform plan that includes regulations on non-resident single-home owners. Metropolitan Seoul-area Democratic lawmakers are demanding a reconsideration of the policy that would impose differential taxation on single-home owners depending on whether they reside in the property or not. Yet the presidential office and Democratic leadership are simply downplaying opposition voices, claiming there are no differing opinions. One cannot help but question whether they are approaching the task of devising measures for residential stability seriously.\n\nThe real estate tax reform has been in turmoil since the government's announcement in August. The plan to reduce the basic deduction on comprehensive real estate tax for non-resident single-home owners to 900 million won was reversed to maintain the current level of 1.2 billion won due to public backlash. In the meantime, public voices emerged demanding exceptions for unavoidable reasons for non-residency, such as children's education, workplace relocation, and caring for ill parents. Meanwhile, the market has already shown side effects, with jeonse and monthly rents surging as non-resident single-home owners move into their properties.\n\nThe presidential office and government are misrepresenting non-resident property holders as the culprits of real estate problems, but even within the Democratic Party, there is recognition that such demonization does not match reality. A comprehensive survey by JoongAng Ilbo of 33 Democratic National Assembly members from Seoul found that 58 percent of respondents (11 out of 19) stated that the government's real estate tax reform plan should be revised to ease the tax burden. They view the government's real estate policy of treating non-resident single-home owners as speculators and punishing them as a factor in losing public support and a risk factor for the general election two years away.\n\nThis is why Metropolitan Democratic lawmakers are arguing that the basic deduction for comprehensive real estate tax should be unified at 1.4 billion won regardless of residency status and that the plan to abolish long-term ownership special deductions should be changed to partially maintain benefits or postpone the abolition timeline. Yesterday, some Democratic lawmakers held a forum on stabilizing jeonse and monthly rents, but lowered their voices when the presidential office emphasized \"one voice\" as the principle of favoring actual residents. A staff member was even spotted trying to cover up the subtitle \"reconsider non-resident single-home regulation\" on the forum's banner. It was a shameful scene.\n\nAt the forum, there was a point made that \"pressure for actual residency on non-resident single-home owners could lead to a decrease in jeonse and monthly rental inventory and price increases.\" A government that claims to support the vulnerable and espouses \"pragmatism\" should certainly listen to such concerns.",
