        "bodyTr": "The Democratic Party announced plans to push through a revised bill next month that is even stricter than its original \"stock price suppression prevention bill\" (amendment to the inheritance and gift tax law). The Democratic Party held a general members' meeting on the 10th and confirmed its plan to process the bill during the regular parliamentary session. The move is largely aimed at consoling discouraged retail investors as stock prices have fallen to the point of threatening the KOSPI 5000 level, which was President Lee Jae-myung's campaign pledge. This is why the opposition is immediately criticizing it as a legislative drive to prop up stock prices. The stock price suppression prevention bill has been pursued on the premise that major shareholders artificially maintain low stock prices to reduce inheritance and gift tax burdens and that such practices should be prevented. The underlying perception is that individual investors suffer losses due to the greed of major shareholders seeking to reduce taxes. Lawmakers including Lee So-young have already proposed an amendment setting the minimum threshold for inheritance tax assessment at 80 percent of net asset value per share if listed stock valuation falls below 80 percent of net asset value per share. The government also stated in August that if the price-to-book ratio (PBR) for at least 12 half-year periods out of the recent 13 half-year periods (six and a half years) falls in the bottom 25 percent by industry for KOSPI and the bottom 10 percent for KOSDAQ, it would be presumed that the company engaged in stock price suppression and would impose taxes by applying a minimum 30 percent markup to current stock prices. However, after President Lee instructed a comprehensive review, saying the revised version \"failed to preserve the original intent of the reform,\" the ruling party came up with a revised bill reducing the target period to three years and raising the markup rate to 40 percent. On the 10th, KOSPI closed at 5239.13, up 4.39 percent from the previous trading day, while KOSDAQ ended at 667.61, up 4.48 percent. Even the existing proposal has faced criticism from some quarters as \"excessive tax burden that denies market value and violates property rights,\" and if the revised bill passes according to the ruling party's hardliners, significant side effects are feared, such as corporate inheritance taxes potentially increasing two to threefold. Business circles argue that cases may arise where the tax amount exceeds the value of the property subject to taxation or where heirs cannot pay taxes even after disposing of all inherited stocks. The very notion that companies can manipulate stock prices, except for some small and medium enterprises with small numbers of issued shares, is anachronistic. The perspective that interprets low PBRs as stock price suppression for tax avoidance is also problematic. Given that the world's highest level of inheritance tax burden already makes business succession difficult, such punitive legislation without addressing the root cause will not raise stock prices.",
